# Clean Engineering Skill

A mandatory guide for writing clean, maintainable, and well-structured code on any project.

---

## ⚡ Pre-Flight Checklist — Answer Before Writing Any File

- [ ] Does this Class/Function have a **single responsibility only**? (SRP)
- [ ] Does this logic already exist somewhere in the project? If yes → **do not repeat it** (DRY)
- [ ] Is this feature actually needed **right now**? If not → **do not build it** (YAGNI)
- [ ] Is the simplest possible solution being used? → **always choose it** (KISS)
- [ ] Does this depend on **abstractions**, not concrete implementations? (DIP)

---

## 1. DRY, KISS, YAGNI

### DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself

Every piece of knowledge must have a single, unambiguous representation in the system.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — duplicated logic
const getUserName = (user) => `${user.firstName} ${user.lastName}`;
const getAdminName = (admin) => `${admin.firstName} ${admin.lastName}`;

// ✅ Good — single source of truth
const getFullName = (person: { firstName: string; lastName: string }): string =>
  `${person.firstName} ${person.lastName}`;
```

### KISS — Keep It Simple, Stupid

The simplest solution that works is always the correct one.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — unnecessary complexity
const isEven = (n: number): boolean => n % 2 === 0 ? true : false;

// ✅ Good — direct and clear
const isEven = (n: number): boolean => n % 2 === 0;
```

### YAGNI — You Ain't Gonna Need It

Do not build features or abstractions that are not required right now.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — building for hypothetical future needs
class UserService {
  createUser() {}
  exportToExcel() {}    // not requested
  generateReport() {}   // not requested
  importFromCSV() {}    // not requested
}

// ✅ Good — only what is needed now
class UserService {
  createUser() {}
}
```

---

## 2. SOLID Principles

### S — Single Responsibility Principle

Every class or module must have **one and only one reason to change**.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — multiple responsibilities in one class
class UserService {
  createUser(data: CreateUserDto) { /* DB logic */ }
  sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) { /* Email logic */ }
  generatePDFReport(user: User) { /* PDF logic */ }
}

// ✅ Good — each class owns one concern
class UserService    { createUser(data: CreateUserDto) {} }
class EmailService   { sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) {} }
class ReportService  { generatePDFReport(user: User) {} }
```

### O — Open/Closed Principle

Software entities should be **open for extension** but **closed for modification**.

```typescript
// ✅ Good — add new payment methods without touching existing code
interface PaymentProcessor {
  process(amount: number): Promise<void>;
}

class StripeProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
  async process(amount: number) { /* ... */ }
}

class TamaraProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
  async process(amount: number) { /* ... */ }  // new method, no existing code changed
}
```

### L — Liskov Substitution Principle

A subclass must be usable wherever its base class is expected, **without breaking the system**.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — subclass breaks the contract
class Rectangle {
  setWidth(w: number)  { this.width = w; }
  setHeight(h: number) { this.height = h; }
}
class Square extends Rectangle {
  setWidth(w: number)  { this.width = w; this.height = w; }  // breaks Rectangle contract
  setHeight(h: number) { this.width = h; this.height = h; }
}

// ✅ Good — separate types, shared interface
interface Shape { area(): number; }
class Rectangle implements Shape { area() { return this.width * this.height; } }
class Square    implements Shape { area() { return this.side ** 2; } }
```

### I — Interface Segregation Principle

No class should be forced to implement interfaces it does not use.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — one fat interface forces unrelated implementations
interface Animal {
  fly(): void;
  swim(): void;
  run(): void;
}

// ✅ Good — small, focused interfaces
interface Flyable  { fly(): void; }
interface Swimmable { swim(): void; }
interface Runnable { run(): void; }

class Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable { fly() {} swim() {} }
class Dog  implements Runnable           { run() {} }
```

### D — Dependency Inversion Principle

Depend on **abstractions**, not on concrete implementations.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — tightly coupled to a specific implementation
class OrderService {
  private db = new PostgresDatabase();  // hard dependency
}

// ✅ Good — depends on an abstraction
interface Database {
  query(sql: string): Promise<unknown>;
}

class OrderService {
  constructor(private db: Database) {}  // any Database implementation works
}
```

---

## 3. Composition over Inheritance

Prefer **composing objects** over building deep inheritance hierarchies.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — fragile inheritance chain
class AdminUser extends PowerUser {}
class PowerUser extends User {}

// ✅ Good — composable behaviors
interface AuthBehavior      { authenticate(): void; }
interface PermissionSet     { can(action: string): boolean; }

class User {
  constructor(
    private auth: AuthBehavior,
    private permissions: PermissionSet,
  ) {}
}
```

---

## 4. Naming Standards

| Context | Convention | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Variables & Functions | camelCase | `getUserById`, `isActive` |
| Classes & Types | PascalCase | `UserService`, `OrderResult` |
| Constants | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `MAX_RETRY_COUNT` |
| Database columns | snake_case | `created_at`, `user_id` |
| React Components | PascalCase | `SearchBar`, `UserCard` |
| API Routes | kebab-case | `/api/user-profile` |

**Golden rule:** The name must answer "what does it do?" without needing a comment.

```typescript
// ❌ Bad — unclear names
const d    = await db.find(id);
const flag = checkUser(u);

// ✅ Good — names that explain themselves
const domain          = await domainRepository.findById(domainId);
const isEmailVerified = await userService.checkEmailVerification(userId);
```

---

## 5. Stack-Specific Rules

### Next.js / TypeScript
- Each page and component has exactly one responsibility
- Business logic lives in `hooks/` — never directly in components
- Shared types defined in `types/` and reused across the project
- Never use `any` except when unavoidable — add a comment explaining why
- API routes follow RESTful conventions in `app/api/`

